1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,772
The question is how much of a miner or streamer we can recreate with

2
00:00:05,772 --> 00:00:07,744
gold, silver and paid options.

3
00:00:07,744 --> 00:00:13,267
Then, with gold exposure targeted to each stock’s past volatility, which investment compounds better

4
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:14,450
across holding periods?

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:18,011
The study expands to fourteen surviving stocks.

6
00:00:18,011 --> 00:00:21,167
Most return tests reach fifteen to seventeen years.

7
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:25,900
Annual filings support a separate valuation study, but their history is shorter.

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,450
These are financed bullion proxies, not verified futures returns.

9
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:33,320
Each expanded model can choose zero extra positions.

10
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:37,926
Its retained, unpenalized training fit cannot be worse than the simpler portfolio.

11
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:42,915
Later performance can still worsen through estimation error, premium costs and changing exposures.

12
00:00:42,915 --> 00:00:44,450
Those are separate tests.

13
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:49,713
Exposure estimates use only earlier data, with a full month gap.

14
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:51,741
Quarterly models share trade dates.

15
00:00:51,741 --> 00:00:53,770
Between trades, quantities stay fixed.

16
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:57,421
Cash earns interest; borrowing and option premiums cost money.

17
00:00:57,421 --> 00:00:59,450
Historical option prices remain hypothetical.

18
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:05,772
For Wheaton, gold and silver with illustrative gold options have a tracking R-squared of

19
00:01:05,772 --> 00:01:07,744
about 0.58 on later data.

20
00:01:07,744 --> 00:01:12,083
These tested options do not consistently improve tracking across the stocks.

21
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:14,450
The remaining return differences are substantial.

22
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:20,524
11 of fourteen stocks lag past-only volatility-targeted gold in their median five-year window.

23
00:01:20,524 --> 00:01:22,553
Franco-Nevada and Wheaton do better.

24
00:01:22,553 --> 00:01:28,233
That supports caution about miners, but overlapping periods and surviving stocks limit how far

25
00:01:28,233 --> 00:01:29,450
we can generalize.

26
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,279
Reported margins are unusually high.

27
00:01:32,279 --> 00:01:37,959
At September prices, Newmont is about eighteen times annual reported earnings; Wheaton is about

28
00:01:37,959 --> 00:01:38,364
forty-one.

29
00:01:38,364 --> 00:01:44,044
Historical high multiples do not give a stable long-term timing rule in this small

30
00:01:44,044 --> 00:01:44,450
sample.

31
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:49,873
World Gold Council estimates show first-half mine output up three percent in twenty twenty-six.

32
00:01:49,873 --> 00:01:53,836
Industry costs rose sixteen percent year over year in the first quarter.

33
00:01:53,836 --> 00:01:55,817
These figures show gradual mine-output growth.

34
00:01:55,817 --> 00:01:59,450
They do not establish that high margins cause future stock underperformance.
